THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS

Lexarii is built only from free software. Every dependency must carry an
AGPL-3.0-compatible licence, and any dependency reaching a client must also
satisfy F-Droid's inclusion rules: free software, no proprietary libraries, no
Google Play Services, reproducibly buildable from source.

This section is regenerated by `make licenses`. Entries below are added as
dependencies are introduced.

Go modules:
  github.com/google/uuid              BSD-3-Clause
  github.com/jackc/pgx/v5             MIT           (+ pgpassfile, pgservicefile, puddle)
  github.com/ledongthuc/pdf           BSD-3-Clause  (Go Authors lineage)
  github.com/pdfcpu/pdfcpu            Apache-2.0    (+ hhrutter/tiff, x/image,
                                                     go-runewidth, spf13/cobra,
                                                     spf13/pflag, go.yaml.in/yaml)
  github.com/microcosm-cc/bluemonday  BSD-3-Clause  (+ golang.org/x/net,
                                                    gorilla/css, douceur, uax29)
  github.com/go-ldap/ldap/v3          MIT           (+ go-asn1-ber, go-ntlmssp)
  github.com/pressly/goose/v3         Apache-2.0    (build-time only, via go run)
  golang.org/x/crypto (argon2id)      BSD-3-Clause
  golang.org/x/sync, x/text, x/net    BSD-3-Clause
  golang.org/x/term                   BSD-3-Clause

  x/term is used by one maintenance command, `make password`, to read a new
  password from the terminal without echoing it. The alternatives were to
  shell out to stty, which is a subprocess and a platform assumption, or to
  print the password on the screen. It is maintained by the Go team and its
  only dependency, x/sys, was already in the build.

  pdfcpu extracts single pages from a PDF, which is what lets a book shared to
  be READ be served page by page instead of handed over as a file. It was
  chosen because it is pure Go: the alternatives with better rendering (MuPDF,
  Poppler) need cgo, and CGO_ENABLED=0 is the reason this ships as one static
  binary. It costs about 3 MB of that binary.

  All AGPL-3.0 compatible. github.com/ledongthuc/pdf is pure Go with no cgo,
  which is what keeps CGO_ENABLED=0 static builds and arm64 cross-compilation
  working - a hard requirement, not a preference.

npm packages:
  SvelteKit, Svelte, Vite toolchain    MIT
  pdfjs-dist (Mozilla)                 Apache-2.0
  playwright-core (Microsoft)          Apache-2.0    (test-only, devDependency)
  @vitest/coverage-v8 (Vitest)         MIT           (test-only, devDependency)

  playwright-core drives the Android app's reader page in a headless
  Chromium for `make android-reader-test`, because the WebView it runs in is
  Chromium and nothing on the JVM can see inside it. It is a devDependency
  with no dependencies of its own, about 13MB installed, and it reaches no
  bundle, no binary and no APK. The code it bundles is MIT, ISC and BSD
  (libwebp's with Google's patent grant), each listed in a lib/*.LICENSE
  file beside its bundle. No browser is vendored: the test uses one it can
  find, or skips.

  @vitest/coverage-v8 reads V8's own coverage counters for
  `npm run test:coverage`, which writes web/coverage/lcov.info; plain
  `npm test` does not load it. It brings sixteen packages, about 7MB
  installed, all MIT except istanbul-lib-coverage, istanbul-lib-report and
  istanbul-reports (BSD-3-Clause) and semver (ISC), and reaches no bundle, no
  binary and no APK.

  All AGPL-3.0 compatible, and all libre - pdfjs-dist in particular is a
  hard requirement for F-Droid, where a proprietary PDF renderer would be an
  outright blocker.

Android (Kotlin) direct dependencies, android/gradle/libs.versions.toml:
  Kotlin stdlib, kotlinx.coroutines,
    kotlinx.serialization              Apache-2.0  (JetBrains)
  Jetpack Compose, AndroidX core,
    lifecycle, activity, DataStore     Apache-2.0  (Google/AndroidX)
  Ktor client (core, OkHttp engine,
    content negotiation, JSON)         Apache-2.0  (JetBrains)
  WorkManager (androidx.work)          Apache-2.0  (Google/AndroidX)

  Ktor's OkHttp engine was chosen over its own native engine because the app
  talks to a self-hosted server as often over plain HTTP on a LAN as over
  TLS, which is the same reasoning `libs.versions.toml` records inline.
  DataStore was chosen over SharedPreferences for the auth token because it
  has a coroutine API with no main-thread disk read.
  WorkManager keeps a book downloading after the app is left and resumes it
  after Android stops the process; it brings Room and SQLite for its own
  queue, all AndroidX and Apache-2.0, and nothing of Play Services.

  The full resolved release runtime classpath - 151 artifacts, every one
  Apache-2.0 or MIT, none proprietary or source-available-only - is audited
  in android/LICENSES-deps.md, with `androidx.profileinstaller` and the
  Kotlin/Compose build tooling specifically addressed since they are the
  ones most likely to draw a reviewer's question. Regenerate that audit with
  `./gradlew :app:dependencies --configuration releaseRuntimeClasspath`
  after any dependency change.

  No Google Play Services, no Firebase, no proprietary SDK anywhere in the
  dependency graph - checked, not assumed, because that is an F-Droid
  inclusion requirement rather than a preference.
